{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>Cursed be he who does not uphold, etc., to do, etc.</b> There is in the verse's meaning an implication that it speaks of the things stated in the passage, and there is in its meaning an implication that it speaks of the entire Torah — and so the Sages of blessed memory explained it (Sotah 37a). According to their words, of blessed memory, the reason it says ״יָקִים לַעֲשׂוֹת״ (״uphold to do״) is that if it had said ״אֲשֶׁר לֹא יָקִים״ (״who does not uphold״) alone, or ״אֲשֶׁר לֹא יַעֲשֶׂה״ (״who does not do״), there would be no standing for the enemies of Israel — for who is this who could uphold all the words of this Torah, and all the more so one who does not have the means in his hand to uphold, such as one who has no field to fulfill the commandments dependent on it, or who has no slave to fulfill the commandments dependent on him. Therefore it says <b>לַעֲשׂוֹת</b> (״to do״) — meaning, he is not included in the curse except one who does not undertake to do them when his hand can reach to do. And therefore it did not say ״אֲשֶׁר לֹא יָקִים״ or ״אֲשֶׁר לֹא יַעֲשֶׂה״ but rather ״אֲשֶׁר לֹא יָקִים לַעֲשׂוֹת״ — meaning, he upholds in his heart the matters to do them when his hand can reach."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:19:30.179787717Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}